/* ---- UnityEngine.Input: the members the game ACTUALLY calls ----
 *
 * Counting `bl` targets in this game's libil2cpp rather than guessing:
 *
 *     Input.get_mousePosition   x4     <- called
 *     Input.GetTouch            x3     <- called
 *     Input.GetMouseButton      x1
 *     Input.get_mousePosition_Injected  ZERO
 *     Input.GetTouch_Injected           ZERO
 *
 * The `_Injected` methods have NO callers. Hooking them (sec.21) hooked dead
 * code, which is why the engine polled our hooks, a finger reached the tracker
 * with correct coordinates, and the game still saw nothing.
 *
 * The reason is visible in get_mousePosition's body: it does not call the
 * managed _Injected method at all. It resolves an INTERNAL CALL BY NAME --
 *
 *     "UnityEngine.Input::get_mousePosition_Injected(UnityEngine.Vector3&)"
 *
 * -- through il2cpp_resolve_icall, caches the pointer, and calls it. That
 * resolves to libunity's NATIVE implementation. The managed _Injected method is
 * a leftover the code generator emitted and nothing uses.
 *
 * So the hook has to go on the wrapper the game calls.
 *
 * RETURN CONVENTIONS, read off the disassembly:
 *   get_mousePosition -> Vector3, three floats, returned as an HFA in s0/s1/s2
 *                        (`ldp s0, s1, [sp] ; ldr s2, [sp, #8]` at the tail).
 *   GetTouch(int)     -> Touch, 0x44 bytes, too large for registers: the caller
 *                        passes an indirect-result pointer in x8 (`mov x19, x8`
 *                        in the prologue) and the index in w0.
 * Both are what a C function returning the same struct types produces. */
#define BMM_RVA_Input_get_mousePosition  0x35d92acu
#define BMM_G3_Input_get_mousePosition   { 0xd10083ffu, 0xa9014ffeu, 0xf00048f3u }
#define BMM_RVA_Input_GetTouch           0x35d8ff0u
#define BMM_G3_Input_GetTouch            { 0xd101c3ffu, 0xa90557feu, 0xa9064ff4u }

