/* ---- EpicLoginScreen: force Play Offline, no user interaction ----
 *
 * This is the screen shown immediately before gameplay, with two buttons:
 * LOGIN WITH EPIC GAMES and PLAY OFFLINE. Offline works; Login softlocks,
 * because it shows `loadingIndicator`, disables both buttons, and waits for
 * EpicSDKManager.Login's callback -- which EOS (neutralised, sec.23) never
 * delivers. Nothing re-enables the buttons, so the screen is a dead end.
 *
 * The interesting part is what PLAY OFFLINE actually does:
 *
 *     OfflineButton_OnClick:  disable both buttons
 *                             strb w8, [x19, #0x88]    ; needHide = 1
 *
 * ...and that is all. The work happens on the next frame, in Update:
 *
 *     +0x038  ldrb w8, [x19, #0x88]     ; needHide
 *     +0x03c  cbz  w8, ret              ; not set -> nothing to do
 *     +0x060  bl   GameManager.startGameFunc
 *     +0x090  b    GUIScreenManager.HideScreen
 *
 * So "play offline" IS "set one bool and let Update start the game". Forcing
 * that is a single word: replace the field load with a constant 1 and the
 * `cbz` can never be taken, so the first Update after the screen appears starts
 * the game and dismisses it.
 *
 * The screen therefore auto-continues offline without being touched, which also
 * makes the Login button unreachable rather than merely harmless -- the softlock
 * cannot be entered at all.
 *
 * Chosen over patching the button handlers because it is one word instead of
 * three, it reuses the game's own offline path exactly as written (start the
 * game, then hide the screen, in that order), and it needs no knowledge of the
 * vtable. Both paths into this instruction converge here: the class-init
 * `tbnz` at Update+0x14 targets 0x3bab6e8, the same address.
 *
 * If the login screen is ever wanted back, set BM_FORCE_OFFLINE to 0 and the
 * EpicLoginPopup patch below still keeps its button from hanging. */
#define BMM_RVA_EpicLoginScreen_needHide  0x3bab6e8u
#define BMM_G1_EpicLoginScreen_needHide   { 0x39422268u }   /* ldrb w8,[x19,#0x88] */
#define BMM_PATCH_EpicLoginScreen_needHide 0x52800028u      /* mov  w8, #1         */

/* ---- EpicLoginPopup.LoginButton_OnClick ----
 *
 * "Log in with Epic Games" softlocks. The handler tail-calls
 * EpicSDKManager.Login(this, callback) and the popup then waits for
 * <LoginButton_OnClick>b__5_0(bool ok) to fire. With EOS neutralised (sec.23)
 * nothing ever calls it back, so the popup stays up forever with no way out.
 *
 * Note the callback would not save us even if EOS were present and simply
 * failed:
 *
 *     b__5_0:  tbz w1, #0, +0x0c    ; ok == false -> fall through to
 *              ldr x8, [x0]         ;                a bare `ret`
 *              ldp x2, x1, [x8, #0x1c8]
 *              br  x2               ; ok == true  -> close the popup
 *
 * It only dismisses the popup on SUCCESS. A failed login leaves it open. So the
 * softlock is the game's own behaviour for "login did not succeed", not
 * something this port introduced -- there is simply no path here where it can.
 *
 * The fix is the sibling button. OfflineButton_OnClick is three instructions:
 *
 *     ldr x8, [x0]                  ; this->klass
 *     ldp x2, x1, [x8, #0x1c8]      ; the SAME vtable slot b__5_0 uses
 *     br  x2                        ; tail-call it: close the popup
 *
 * Copying those three words verbatim over LoginButton_OnClick makes the Epic
 * button behave as the Offline button: the popup dismisses and play continues.
 * That is the honest outcome -- with no EOS the login cannot succeed, and
 * "dismiss and carry on offline" is what the game itself does for that case via
 * the button sitting next to it.
 *
 * Verbatim matters: the replacement is not hand-assembled, it is the three
 * words already present at OfflineButton_OnClick in this same binary, so the
 * vtable slot and calling convention cannot drift apart.
 *
 * LoginButton_OnClick spans 176 bytes (next symbol is OfflineButton_OnClick),
 * so a 12-byte rewrite sits well inside it. */
#define BMM_RVA_EpicLoginPopup_Login   0x3c2553cu
#define BMM_G3_EpicLoginPopup_Login    { 0xa9bd5ffeu, 0xa90157f6u, 0xa9024ff4u }
/* Byte-for-byte OfflineButton_OnClick @0x3c255ec. */
